WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.947
A lock protects one row.

00:00:05.947 --> 00:00:09.895
But a transaction holds many locks across its life.

00:00:09.895 --> 00:00:13.842
Release one early, and another transaction slips into the gap and reads work you might still undo.

00:00:13.842 --> 00:00:17.790
The rule that prevents that is two-phase locking.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:21.623
Locks are the primitive.

00:00:21.623 --> 00:00:25.457
The protocol for when to acquire and release them is two-phase locking.

00:00:25.457 --> 00:00:29.290
The rule is two sentences.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:33.123
Growing phase, you may acquire locks, but release none.

00:00:33.123 --> 00:00:36.957
Shrinking phase, you may release locks, but acquire none.

00:00:36.957 --> 00:00:40.790
The first release flips growing to shrinking, and you can never acquire again.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:44.624
The chart plots the number of locks held against time.

00:00:44.624 --> 00:00:48.457
Up the staircase through the growing phase, lock by lock.

00:00:48.457 --> 00:00:52.290
Hold at the peak, everything the transaction will ever need.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:56.124
Then down through the shrinking phase, one release at a time.

00:00:56.124 --> 00:00:59.957
That single rule earns the conflict-serializability we proved, with no schedule to check.

00:00:59.957 --> 00:01:04.880
Strict two-phase locking, S 2 PL, takes the rule one step further.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.802
Release no locks at all during execution.

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:14.725
The growing phase climbs as before, but the shrinking phase is a single drop at the very end, when the transaction commits.

00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:19.647
Reed the chart the same way, number of locks on the y axis, time on the x.

00:01:19.647 --> 00:01:24.570
Locks rise, then plateau through the body of the transaction, then fall to zero in one step at COMMIT.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:29.492
Why the stricter version?

00:01:29.492 --> 00:01:34.415
Because of a problem that only shows up if you release locks early.

00:01:34.415 --> 00:01:37.677
That problem is the cascading abort.

00:01:37.677 --> 00:01:40.940
Under regular two-phase locking, T1 writes a row and unlocks early.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.202
T2 reeds that uncommitted value and builds on it.

00:01:44.202 --> 00:01:47.465
Then T1 aborts.

00:01:47.465 --> 00:01:50.727
T2 has to abort too, because it trusted work that never happened.

00:01:50.727 --> 00:01:53.989
One abort cascades into another.

00:01:53.989 --> 00:01:57.252
Strict 2 PL prevents this by construction.

00:01:57.252 --> 00:02:00.514
Nobody reeds your work until you have committed it.

00:02:00.514 --> 00:02:03.793
Two-phase locking earns the conflict-serializability we proved, just by the rule.

00:02:03.793 --> 00:02:07.071
Strict 2 PL ships in Postgres and Oracle.

00:02:07.071 --> 00:02:10.350
But locks add one new risk.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.628
Two transactions each waiting on the other forever.

00:02:13.628 --> 00:02:16.907
Next, microschedules and deadlock.
